# ---------------------------------------------------------------- 2
A(('h1', S2))
A(('p', '2.1 The scene in Luke 11. “And it came to pass, that, as he was praying in a certain place, when he ceased, one of his disciples said unto him, Lord, teach us to pray, as John also taught his disciples” (11:1). Luke does not name the place. The verses before it have Jesus in a village at the house of Martha (10:38–42), and Luke does not say that 11:1 is the same village. One disciple asks. Jesus answers with a prayer: “When ye pray, say, Our Father which art in heaven” (11:2). The prayer includes “Thy kingdom come” (11:2), “Give us day by day our daily bread” (11:3) and a petition about forgiveness and temptation (11:4).'))
A(('p', '2.2 The turn to the friend. The next verse reads, “And he said unto them, Which of you shall have a friend, and shall go unto him at midnight” (11:5). The request came from one disciple, and Luke writes “unto them.” He does not say how many were there or narrow “them” further. No question or remark comes between the prayer and the story, and Luke gives no statement of purpose before it. What Jesus says the story means comes after it, in His own words (11:8–13).'))
A(('p', '2.3 The scene in Luke 18. Luke 17 reports a question from the Pharisees: “And when he was demanded of the Pharisees, when the kingdom of God should come, he answered them and said” (17:20). Luke then writes, “And he said unto the disciples, The days will come, when ye shall desire to see one of the days of the Son of man, and ye shall not see it” (17:22). The saying continues in the verses that follow. Chapter 18 opens, “And he spake a parable unto them to this end, that men ought always to pray, and not to faint” (18:1). Luke does not narrow “them” to the Pharisees or to the disciples. He gives the end for which the parable was spoken in the same verse that introduces it.'))
A(('call', 'Each Account Stands in Its Own Setting',
   'The friend at midnight follows a disciple’s request to be taught to pray (Luke 11:1). The judge and the widow follow a saying about the days of the Son of man and carry Luke’s own statement of purpose (Luke 18:1). Both concern prayer, and neither is read into the other.'))
A(('p', '2.4 The prophetic context. Jesus’ ministry in the Gospels is a ministry in which He says He was sent to preach the kingdom. In Luke He says, “I must preach the kingdom of God to other cities also: for therefore am I sent” (4:43). In Matthew He preached, “Repent: for the kingdom of heaven is at hand” (4:17). The prayer Jesus gives in Luke 11 asks, “Thy kingdom come,” and Luke 18:8 speaks of the coming of the Son of man. The stories are read as spoken in that ministry and not as sayings that float free of it.'))
A(('p', '2.5 Why Jesus tells them here. In Luke 11, Jesus tells the story after He has taught the prayer and in answer to the setting of a disciple’s request to be taught to pray. In Luke 18, Luke says why: “that men ought always to pray, and not to faint.” The two occasions are not the same, and neither is read into the other.'))
