A(('p', '4.3 What Jesus does not name in the stories. Jesus names importunity, asking, seeking, knocking, evil fathers who give good gifts, the heavenly Father, the Holy Spirit and those who ask, in Luke 11. He names the unjust judge, God, His own elect who cry day and night, the Son of man and faith on the earth, in Luke 18. He leaves the parts of the stories without an identification. Jesus does not identify the man who goes at midnight. Jesus does not identify the one within. Jesus does not identify the friend who came on a journey. Jesus does not identify the three loaves, the door or the children in bed. Jesus does not identify the judge beyond calling him unjust, and He does not identify the city. Jesus does not identify the widow or her adversary. Jesus does not state in so many words that the one within is the heavenly Father of 11:13; He says “If ye then, being evil” and “how much more.” Jesus does not state that the judge is God, and He does not state that the widow is “his own elect.” He sets the judge and God in a question and names the elect only as those “which cry day and night unto him.” He does not say who they are beyond those words. Jesus does not say who the adversary is.'))
A(('call', 'A Warning Against Adding',
   'Because Jesus names only importunity, asking, seeking, knocking, the heavenly Father, the Holy Spirit, the unjust judge, God, His own elect, the Son of man and faith on the earth, nothing further is named here. A reader who says that the one within is a certain person, that the loaves, the door or the adversary stands for something, that the widow is a certain people, or that the judge is a figure of God is supplying what the Lord did not.'))
A(('p', '4.4 The explanations stay with their accounts. The words of Luke 11:8–13 belong to the friend, and the words of Luke 18:6–8 belong to the judge and the widow. Both accounts concern prayer, by the setting of the first (11:1) and by Luke’s statement of the second (18:1). A shared subject does not make them one, and neither account’s words supply what the other leaves unnamed. “The heavenly Father” of Luke 11:13 and “God” of Luke 18:7 are separate sayings in separate settings, and Jesus does not join them.'))

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- 5
A(('h1', S5))
A(('p', 'This section states the meaning that arises from the comparisons themselves. It repeats none of Jesus’ identifications from Section 4 and assigns an identity to no part of the stories.'))
A(('p', '5.1 The comparison of the friend. In Luke 11 the comparison is put as a question: which of them would go to a friend at midnight for three loaves and, refused at the door, still be given what he needs? Jesus says that though the friend will not rise because of friendship, he will rise because of importunity. The meaning of the comparison is in the movement of the story: a need, an asking, a refusal, a persistence and a giving.'))
A(('p', '5.2 What the friend comparison communicates.'))
A(('bul', [
    '**The need is brought to the door.** The man goes at midnight with a plain reason, and the story does not describe him asking indirectly.',
    '**A refusal is not the end.** The one within says the door is shut and he cannot rise, and Jesus says that importunity moves him to rise and give.',
    '**The giving matches the need.** He gives “as many as he needeth,” not the three loaves only.',
    '**Jesus turns the story to asking.** After the story He says, “Ask, and it shall be given you; seek, and ye shall find; knock, and it shall be opened unto you,” and sets a son who asks a father beside it.',
    '**The giving of a good Father is put beyond the giving of evil fathers.** In His own “how much more” Jesus sets what evil fathers know how to give beside what the heavenly Father gives to them that ask.',
]))
A(('p', '5.3 What the judge and widow comparison communicates. The second comparison tells of one who asks and is refused for a while, and who keeps coming. The judge, who feared not God and regarded not man, gives way because the widow troubles him and her continual coming would weary him. Jesus draws a question from the story, “shall not God avenge his own elect, which cry day and night unto him,” and answers that He will avenge them speedily. The story ends with a question that Jesus leaves open: “when the Son of man cometh, shall he find faith on the earth?”'))
A(('bul', [
    '**Continual coming is not wasted.** The judge does what he would not do at first, and the story gives the cause: her coming.',
    '**An unjust giver is set beside God.** Jesus calls the judge unjust and puts God in a question about avenging; the comparison runs from the one to the other and does not make them alike.',
    '**Crying day and night is described.** Jesus says His own elect “cry day and night unto him,” and that He bears long with them.',
    '**The close is a question about faith.** Jesus asks whether, when the Son of man comes, He will find faith on the earth. The question is asked and the text gives no answer to it.',
]))
A(('p', '5.4 What the hearers were meant to understand. In Luke 11 the hearers were the disciples with the one who had asked to be taught to pray. They were meant to understand that asking is met and that persistence at a closed door is answered, and they hear Jesus say that the heavenly Father gives the Holy Spirit to them that ask him. In Luke 18 Luke names the end of the parable: “that men ought always to pray, and not to faint.” The hearers were meant to understand that continual asking is not given up, and the closing question about faith asks them to weigh their own. These are comparisons about prayer in the Kingdom ministry, and they are not about the church of the Body.'))
A(('p', '5.5 What this section does not do. It does not say who the man, the one within, the judge or the widow is, or what the loaves, the door, the adversary or “a while” is. It does not say who “his own elect” are beyond Jesus’ words. It does not turn asking, seeking, knocking or continual coming into steps by which a person is saved. It says nothing about when the Son of man comes, and it draws no meaning from another parable into these stories.'))

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- 6
A(('h1', S6))
A(('p', 'The friend at midnight belongs to Luke 11, and the judge and the widow belong to Luke 18. They are kept apart here from three neighbors.'))
A(('tbl', '', [
    ['Luke 18:9–14', '“he spake this parable unto certain which trusted in themselves that they were righteous”', 'The Pharisee and the Tax Collector. It follows at once in Luke 18, with a new introduction at 18:9, its own hearers and its own study. The widow is not read into it and it is not read into her.'],
    ['Luke 17:7–10', '“which of you, having a servant plowing or feeding cattle”', 'The Unprofitable Servants. Told in Luke 17, before the saying about the days of the Son of man, after the apostles ask, “Increase our faith.” It has its own study and is not a story of asking.'],
    ['Luke 14:16', '“A certain man made a great supper, and bade many”', 'The Great Banquet. Told at a table in Luke 14 about a supper and excuses. It is not a story of a request at a door.'],
], [0.17, 0.33, 0.50]))
A(('p', '6.1 Differences in setting. The friend at midnight is told after a disciple’s request to be taught to pray and after the prayer itself (Luke 11:1–4). The judge and the widow are told after the saying about the days of the Son of man, and Luke gives their purpose in the verse that introduces them (18:1). The story that follows in Luke 18 begins, “And he spake this parable unto certain which trusted in themselves that they were righteous, and despised others” (18:9). It is a new introduction with its own hearers, and each story is read in its own place.'))
A(('p', '6.2 Differences in characters. The friend story has a man, a friend who has come on a journey, one within, three loaves, a shut door and children in bed. The judge story has a judge, a widow and an adversary. The stories that follow and precede have servants, a householder, a Pharisee and a publican. No character is carried from one account to another.'))
A(('p', '6.3 Differences in narrative movement. The friend moves from a need to a refusal to a persistence to a giving, and then to Jesus’ words about asking and a Father’s giving. The judge moves from a refusal “for a while” to a giving, and then to Jesus’ question about God and His own elect and a question about faith. The Unprofitable Servants moves from a servant’s duty to a saying about duty done. The Great Banquet moves from a supper to excuses to a widened invitation. None of those movements is read into the friend or the widow.'))
A(('p', '6.4 Differences in interpretive detail. Three details must not be carried across. First, “your heavenly Father” of Luke 11:13 and “God” of Luke 18:7 are different wordings in different accounts, and neither is used to explain the other. Second, Matthew 7:7–11 gives the same sayings about asking, seeking and knocking in the Sermon on the Mount, and it ends “how much more shall your Father which is in heaven give good things to them that ask him?” where Luke 11:13 ends “give the Holy Spirit to them that ask him.” The two wordings differ, each stands in its own setting, and no conclusion is drawn from the difference. Third, “his own elect” in Luke 18:7 is Jesus’ phrase in that saying, and the word “elect” in other sayings (for example Matthew 24:22, 24, 31) belongs to those settings. Jesus does not join them to Luke 18:7.'))

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- 7
A(('h1', S7))
A(('p', '7.1 The original address. In Luke 11, Jesus speaks to His disciples after one of them asks to be taught to pray (11:1). In Luke 18, Luke writes “unto them” and gives the end of the parable (18:1), after a saying to the disciples about the days of the Son of man (17:22). Both belong to the earthly ministry in which He said He was sent to preach the kingdom of God (Luke 4:43) and preached that the kingdom of heaven is at hand (Matthew 4:17), and the prayer Jesus gives in Luke 11 asks, “Thy kingdom come.”'))
A(('call', 'Standing Statements',
   'Jesus speaks during His earthly Kingdom ministry before the Acts overlap. The Mystery of the Body of Christ begins with Paul’s Acts 9 calling. This is a principled lesson, not a transfer of Kingdom identity to the Body.'))
A(('p', '7.2 The Acts overlap boundary. Israel’s prophetic program did not end with the Gospels, and it did not stop when Paul was called. The Kingdom message is still preached to Israel in Acts 1–8, and through Acts 9–28 Israel’s program and the Mystery run side by side. The two never merge, transfer or absorb one another. Israel’s program was nationally suspended at Acts 28. The friend and the widow were told before any of that, and they are not read as though the church of the Body were already present.'))
A(('p', '7.3 The Mystery begins with Paul. The Mystery of the Body of Christ begins with Paul’s Acts 9 calling. Nothing in Luke 11 or Luke 18 speaks of the Body of Christ, and neither passage is read as though the Body were already formed. The Mystery that was hid in God and made known through Paul is read in Paul’s letters.'))
A(('p', '7.4 What is not transferred. The asking, the seeking, the knocking, the continual coming and the avenging are not a description of the Body of Christ. The stories are not read as a universal picture of how any person is heard by God. The gift named in Luke 11:13 is reported as Jesus said it in His earthly ministry, and no teaching on how the Holy Spirit is given or on present practice is built from it here. The avenging of Luke 18:7–8 and the coming of the Son of man are not the Body’s promise. “Ask,” “seek,” “knock” and “come” are actions in stories and in sayings, and they are not a mechanism of salvation. The Body’s salvation is read in Paul’s letters and not in these stories: “For by grace are ye saved through faith; and that not of yourselves: it is the gift of God: Not of works, lest any man should boast” (Ephesians 2:8–9).'))
A(('p', '7.5 Responsible present learning. A reader today may draw lessons from what the comparisons show. They are lessons of conduct and not of identity.'))
A(('bul', [
    '**A lesson of persistence.** The man goes to the door and does not leave, and the widow keeps coming. Luke gives the end as “that men ought always to pray, and not to faint.” A reader learns not to give up asking.',
    '**A lesson of plain asking.** The man says what he needs and why. A reader learns to put a need into words.',
    '**A lesson of patience with delay.** Both asked and were refused for a time. A reader learns that a delay is not the end of the matter in the stories.',
    '**A lesson of weighing the one asked.** A reluctant friend and an unjust judge give way, and Jesus sets a good giver beyond them. A reader learns to consider the character of the one he asks.',
    '**A lesson of weighing one’s own faith.** Jesus closes with a question about faith. A reader learns to put that question to himself and not to answer it for others.',
]))
A(('p', 'None of these lessons says that the Body is the widow, that the Body is the elect, or that the Body is the one who asks at the door. They are principles drawn from what the stories show.'))

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- 8
A(('h1', S8))
A(('p', 'Jesus tells of a man who goes to a friend at midnight for three loaves, is told the door is shut and the children are in bed, and is given what he needs because of his importunity. He says, “Ask, and it shall be given you; seek, and ye shall find; knock, and it shall be opened unto you,” and closes with “how much more shall your heavenly Father give the Holy Spirit to them that ask him?” (Luke 11:5–13). He tells, with Luke’s statement that men ought always to pray and not to faint, of a widow who asks an unjust judge, “Avenge me of mine adversary,” and is refused for a while and then heard because of her continual coming. He asks whether God will not avenge His own elect which cry day and night unto him, says that He will avenge them speedily, and ends with a question about faith on the earth (Luke 18:1–8). Jesus names nothing else in the stories. He spoke during His earthly Kingdom ministry before the Acts overlap, and the Mystery of the Body of Christ begins with Paul’s Acts 9 calling. Any reading that says who the man, the friend, the judge or the widow is, or that makes asking, seeking, knocking or continual coming a way of being saved, says more than the text says.'))
A(('pk', '**Teaching Summary.**'))
A(('bul', [
    '**The sequence:** a need at midnight, a refusal at the door and a giving because of importunity, followed by sayings about asking; a refusal “for a while” and a giving because of continual coming, followed by a question about God and a question about faith.',
    '**The meaning:** asking that does not give up is met, and the giving of a good Father is put beyond the giving of evil fathers and of an unjust judge.',
    '**The boundaries:** only importunity, asking, seeking, knocking, the heavenly Father, the Holy Spirit, the unjust judge, God, His own elect, the Son of man and faith on the earth are named; the man, the friend, the loaves, the door, the widow and the adversary are not.',
    '**The present lesson:** persistence, plain asking, patience with delay, weighing the one asked and weighing one’s own faith, drawn as principles and not as a transfer.',
]))

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- 9
A(('h1', S9))
A(('bul', [
    '**Between what Jesus says and what He leaves.** He speaks of importunity, asking, seeking, knocking, the heavenly Father, the Holy Spirit, the unjust judge, God, His own elect and faith on the earth. He does not explain the man, the friend, the one within, the loaves, the door, the widow or the adversary.',
    '**Between the two stories.** The friend is asked for loaves at a door and the judge is asked to avenge. The friend is called “friend” and the judge “unjust.” The second is not a further identification of the first.',
    '**Between the two settings.** The first follows a disciple’s request to be taught to pray. The second carries Luke’s statement of purpose, “that men ought always to pray, and not to faint.”',
    '**Between characters.** The one within is not said to be the heavenly Father, the judge is not said to be God, and the widow is not said to be the elect.',
    '**Between neighbors.** The Pharisee and the Tax Collector, the Unprofitable Servants and the Great Banquet belong to their own studies.',
    '**What must not be assigned.** No identity for the man, the friend, the one within, the judge, the widow, the adversary or the elect beyond Jesus’ own words. The Body of Christ is neither the widow nor the elect.',
    '**What must not be imported.** No meaning carried in from the Pharisee and the Tax Collector, the Unprofitable Servants, the Great Banquet, Matthew 7:7–11 or the sayings about the elect in Matthew 24.',
    '**What must not be made of the actions.** Asking, seeking, knocking and coming are actions in stories and in sayings. They are not steps by which a person is saved, and no teaching on the Holy Spirit or on present practice is built from Luke 11:13.',
]))

