A(('p', '13.2 Neighboring parables. The Pharisee and the Tax Collector, the Unprofitable Servants and the Great Banquet are each read in their own study of the Parable Studies. These stories make no use of their meanings, and they make none of these.'))
A(('p', '13.3 Final Synthesis. Jesus tells of a man who goes to a friend at midnight for three loaves and is given what he needs because of his importunity, and of a widow who comes to an unjust judge until he avenges her. He says to ask, seek and knock, names the heavenly Father who gives the Holy Spirit to them that ask him, and asks whether God will not avenge His own elect, which cry day and night unto him. He names nothing else in the stories, and Luke 18:8 ends with a question about faith on the earth.'))
A(('pk', 'Write the synthesis in your own words in five to seven sentences. In it:'))
A(('bul', [
    'Tell the friend and the widow in their order.',
    'State exactly what Jesus says in Luke 11:8–13 and Luke 18:6–8 and nothing beyond it.',
    'Say plainly which parts of the stories Jesus does not identify.',
    'State what the comparisons say about asking that does not give up.',
    'State when Jesus told them and what He was sent to preach, and one principle of conduct for today.',
    'End with one sentence naming what the passage does not say.',
]))
A(('call', 'Boundaries to Remember',
   'Jesus names importunity, asking, seeking, knocking, the heavenly Father, the Holy Spirit, the unjust judge, God, His own elect, the Son of man and faith on the earth, and nothing more. No identity for the man, the friend, the one within, the judge, the widow or the adversary. No symbol carried in from another parable. Asking, seeking, knocking and coming are actions in stories, not steps of salvation. The Body of Christ is neither the widow nor the elect. The lessons are principles of conduct, not a transfer of Kingdom identity to the Body.'))
